// Physics U1 L4 — Force, Mass, and Acceleration

::PHY-U1-L4-Q01::Which statement best describes inertia? {=An object’s resistance to a change in its motion.#Correct: inertia resists changes in velocity. ~A force that always slows an object.#Friction may slow objects, but it is not inertia. ~The rate at which velocity changes.#That is acceleration. ~The product of force and time.#That is impulse.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q02::Which SI unit measures force? {=newton (N)#Correct. ~kilogram (kg)#Kilogram measures mass. ~meter per second (m/s)#This measures speed. ~joule (J)#Joule measures energy.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q03::A 4 kg object has a net force of 12 N. What is its acceleration? {=3 m/s²#Correct: a = 12/4. ~0.33 m/s²#The ratio was inverted. ~8 m/s²#Force and mass were subtracted. ~48 m/s²#Force and mass were multiplied.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q04::What net force accelerates a 5 kg object at 2 m/s²? {=10 N#Correct: F = ma. ~2.5 N#Mass was divided by acceleration. ~7 N#Mass and acceleration were added. ~0.4 N#Acceleration was divided by mass.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q05::If net force stays constant and mass doubles, acceleration {=is cut in half.#Correct: a = F/m. ~doubles.#Acceleration is inversely proportional to mass. ~stays the same.#Changing mass changes acceleration at fixed force. ~becomes zero.#It decreases but does not necessarily become zero.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q06::If mass stays constant and net force triples, acceleration {=triples.#Correct: acceleration is proportional to net force. ~is divided by three.#That reverses the relationship. ~stays the same.#Force changes acceleration. ~increases by 9 times.#The relationship is linear, not squared.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q07::An object moves at constant velocity. Its net force is {=zero.#Correct: constant velocity means zero acceleration. ~equal to its mass.#Mass is not a force. ~in the direction of motion.#A net force would change velocity. ~necessarily downward.#Vertical forces may balance.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q08::Two horizontal forces, 15 N right and 9 N left, act on an object. Net force is {=6 N right.#Correct: subtract opposite forces. ~24 N right.#Opposite forces are not added as magnitudes. ~6 N left.#The larger force is rightward. ~zero.#The forces are unequal.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q09::Newton’s first law applies when {=net force is zero and motion remains unchanged.#Correct. ~only an object is at rest.#It also applies to constant-velocity motion. ~mass equals acceleration.#Those quantities have different units. ~every object accelerates.#Acceleration requires nonzero net force.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q10::Which equation is Newton’s second law? {=F_net = ma#Correct. ~p = mv#This defines momentum. ~v = d/t#This defines average speed. ~W = Fd#This relates work to force and displacement.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q11::A 20 N net force produces 4 m/s² acceleration. The mass is {=5 kg#Correct: m = F/a. ~80 kg#Force and acceleration were multiplied. ~24 kg#They were added. ~0.2 kg#Acceleration was divided by force.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q12::Newton’s third-law forces {=act on different objects and are equal in magnitude, opposite in direction.#Correct. ~act on the same object and cancel.#They act on different objects. ~exist only during collisions.#They occur in every interaction. ~have different magnitudes when masses differ.#Their magnitudes are equal.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q13::A small student and a large student push apart. The forces they exert are {=equal, while the smaller student has greater acceleration.#Correct: equal interaction forces, different a = F/m. ~larger on the larger student.#Third-law forces are equal. ~equal, with equal accelerations.#Different masses yield different accelerations. ~zero after they move.#The interaction force acts during the push.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q14::Which is a valid third-law pair? {=Earth pulls on a ball and the ball pulls on Earth.#Correct: two forces, two objects. ~Weight and normal force on a book.#Both act on the same object. ~Force and acceleration of one cart.#Acceleration is not a force. ~Mass and inertia of a person.#Neither is a force pair.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q15::A book rests on a table. Why does it not accelerate vertically? {=The upward normal force balances its downward weight.#Correct. ~It has no weight.#Gravity still acts. ~The table exerts no force.#The table provides a normal force. ~Its mass becomes zero.#Mass does not vanish.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q16::Momentum is calculated by {=p = mv.#Correct. ~p = ma.#That resembles force, not momentum. ~p = F/m.#That calculates acceleration. ~p = v/m.#Momentum increases with mass, not inversely.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q17::Which object has greatest momentum? {=A 4 kg object moving 5 m/s.#Correct: p = 20 kg·m/s. ~A 2 kg object moving 8 m/s.#Its momentum is 16. ~A 10 kg object moving 1 m/s.#Its momentum is 10. ~A 3 kg object moving 4 m/s.#Its momentum is 12.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q18::The direction of momentum is always {=the direction of velocity.#Correct: mass is scalar and positive. ~the direction of acceleration.#Acceleration can differ from velocity. ~opposite the net force.#Net force changes momentum. ~vertical.#It depends on motion.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q19::On an acceleration-versus-net-force graph for constant mass, the trend should be {=a straight line through the origin.#Correct: a = F/m. ~a horizontal line.#Acceleration changes with force. ~an inverse curve.#That describes acceleration versus mass. ~a circle.#No circular relation applies.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q20::In an experiment testing how force affects acceleration, which variable should remain constant? {=mass#Correct: control mass to isolate force. ~net force#Net force is the independent variable. ~acceleration#Acceleration is measured. ~time only#Time may be measured, but mass is the essential controlled variable.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q21::A model gives a = 6 m/s², but measured acceleration is 5.4 m/s². A likely explanation is {=friction or other unmodeled resistive forces.#Correct. ~Newton’s second law is invalid.#The discrepancy reflects model conditions. ~mass has no effect.#Mass directly affects acceleration. ~units do not matter.#Units remain essential.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q22::Which change can produce acceleration? {=A change in speed or direction.#Correct: velocity includes both. ~Only a change in speed.#Direction changes also accelerate. ~Only an increase in speed.#Slowing also accelerates. ~Constant velocity.#Constant velocity has zero acceleration.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q23::A 3 kg cart accelerates 2 m/s² right while friction is 1 N left. The applied force is {=7 N right.#Correct: applied − 1 = 6, so applied = 7. ~6 N right.#That is the net force. ~5 N right.#That would give 4 N net. ~1 N right.#That only matches friction magnitude.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q24::Why are mass and weight not interchangeable? {=Mass measures inertia; weight is gravitational force.#Correct. ~Both are measured in newtons.#Mass is measured in kilograms. ~Weight never changes.#Weight can change with gravitational field. ~Mass is a vector.#Mass is scalar.}

::PHY-U1-L4-Q25::Which explanation correctly combines Newton’s laws? {=An unbalanced force changes motion; acceleration equals net force divided by mass; interactions produce equal opposite forces on different objects.#Correct synthesis. ~Objects need force to keep moving at constant velocity.#That contradicts the first law. ~Larger objects always exert larger interaction forces.#Third-law force magnitudes are equal. ~Balanced forces cause acceleration.#Balanced forces give zero net force.}
